1. rg NEVER ASKS THE LOCALE
   grep takes its definition of a character from LC_CTYPE, so the same
   pattern gives different answers in different environments. rg has no
   locale code path at all: patterns and haystacks are UTF-8 by
   default, everywhere, on every platform. One implementation, one
   answer -- which is the single biggest reason to reach for it.

2. IT READS THE BOM, WHICH IS WHY IT CAN SEARCH UTF-16
   utf-8, no mark         63 61 66 c3  -> nothing; searched as raw bytes
   utf-8 + signature      ef bb bf 63  -> UTF-8
   utf-16 + BOM           ff fe 63 00  -> UTF-16LE
   utf-16-le, NO mark     63 00 61 00  -> nothing; searched as raw bytes
   utf-32 + BOM           ff fe 00 00  -> UTF-32LE mark -- and rg tests only three, so it reads this as UTF-16LE

   Three marks are tested, and UTF-32 is not one of them. A UTF-16 file
   WITHOUT a BOM is also searched as raw bytes -- so 'rg reads UTF-16'
   is true only of files that announce themselves.

3. --column COUNTS BYTES, AND SAYS SO
   'café x'
   the x is character 6, and byte 7
   rg reports the byte offset. That is the honest number for a tool
   that works in bytes, and it is not the number an editor's cursor
   shows -- so a script that jumps to rg's column lands one place early
   on any line containing a multi-byte character.

4. WHAT IT SKIPS BY DEFAULT, WHICH IS NOT AN ENCODING QUESTION
   rg respects .gitignore, skips hidden files and skips binary files
   unless told otherwise. Those are three separate defaults, three
   separate flags (-u, -uu, -uuu stack them off), and every one of them
   can make a search come back empty on a file that is right there.
   grep has none of these defaults, which is why the two tools
   disagreeing usually has nothing to do with the pattern.
